it's a common sight a truck traveling  along the highway  but the drivers not steering like in a  science fiction film at daimler the  future of trucking has already begun  this demonstration as part of a  technology event on an unopened motorway  section near magdeburg has caused a  sensation in freight haulage Wolfgang  ban heart head of Daimler Trucks says  the mercedes-benz future truck 2025  represents the company's vision for  transport and long-distance services a  decade from now for years Daimler has  been one of the leading companies in  active and passive driving assistance  systems for both cars and trucks with  safety systems including proximity  control assist stop and go assist active  brake assist 3 and Lane Keeping Assist  these trucks already offer a high level  of safety but what is now being  demonstrated goes even further  that I got a man hug says this is a  world premiere a truck that can drive  autonomously that means it can drive oh  my itself at 80 km/h without the driver  having to steer it can accelerate or  break stays right in the lane and is  very very safe autonomous driving is  made possible by networking and  augmenting existing technology with  cameras and radar sensors when the  message appears on the display the  driver can activate the system and turn  his seat 45 degrees away from the  steering wheel thanks to the so-called  highway pilot the truck can now travel  without driver input this system can  adjust to a wide variety of driving  situations for example if an emergency  vehicle approaches it can automatically  pull over to the right to allow it to  pass and then return to the lane without  driver intervention the driver can still  take over at any time but how do  truckers feel about that sis Terrance  patent hunts roofed is a truck driver he  says the new system is good and less  stressful once you get used to it it  takes some time to get used to letting  go of the steering wheel and taking your  eye off the road by letting the truck  take care of the driving he says he has  time to take care of other activities in  the cattlemen fallen infatti booted Curt  Hutton for house under today guidance  attune cameras and radar sensors around  the vehicle enable drivers to pay  attention to other things but there's no  word from Daimler as to what this  technology will cost autonomous driving  is ready for use in everyday trucking  operations freight haulers that watch  the road promise to make long-distance  transport safer
 

